select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-001","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What is the difference between commercial and non-commercial energy? Give an example of each.","back":"Commercial energy is traded at a market price (coal, oil, natural gas, grid electricity); non-commercial energy is not normally bought and sold (firewood, cattle dung, agricultural waste).","explain":"Commercial sources power industry, transport and urban households and are the basis of national energy statistics. Non-commercial sources are gathered free, mainly in rural areas, and are often left out of official consumption figures. Solar energy used for water heating is also treated as non-commercial in BEE questions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S7; BEE 2025 P1 Q44","order":1000},
{"id":"p1-002","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish primary and secondary energy sources.","back":"Primary energy is found or stored in nature (coal, crude oil, natural gas, biomass, solar, wind, hydro, nuclear fuel); secondary energy is produced by converting primary energy (electricity, steam, petrol/diesel from refining).","explain":"Primary sources are mostly converted in industrial utilities into secondary forms before end use. For example, coal is burnt in a power station to make electricity, and crude oil is refined into transport fuels. Every conversion step has losses, so saving one unit of secondary energy saves more than one unit of primary energy.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1001},
{"id":"p1-003","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Distinguish renewable and non-renewable energy, with an example of each.","back":"Renewable energy comes from sources that are naturally replenished and practically inexhaustible (solar, wind, hydro, biomass, geothermal); non-renewable energy comes from finite stocks that run out over time (coal, oil, natural gas, nuclear fuel).","explain":"Renewables are generally low-carbon and do not deplete. Fossil fuels took millions of years to form and are being used up much faster than they form. Coal is the standard 'non-renewable' answer in BEE objective questions.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q43, S7","order":1002},
{"id":"p1-004","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How is the Reserves-to-Production (R/P) ratio calculated, and what does it tell you?","back":"R/P = reserves remaining at the end of the year ÷ production in that year. It gives the number of years the remaining reserves would last if production continued at that rate.","explain":"India's coal R/P ratio is much higher than its oil and gas R/P ratios. That is why coal is seen as the fuel that can give secure domestic supply for decades, while oil and gas depend heavily on imports. The ratio changes as new reserves are found or production rises.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q34; BEE 2025 P1 Q34","order":1003},
{"id":"p1-005","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Define energy intensity.","back":"Energy intensity = Energy consumption ÷ GDP (for example, toe per million US$ of GDP). It shows how much energy the economy uses to produce one unit of economic output.","explain":"High energy intensity means energy is being turned into GDP inefficiently, or that the economy relies on energy-heavy industry. Low energy intensity usually means energy is being turned into GDP efficiently. It is a macro indicator used to compare countries over time.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q23; BEE 2025 P1 S2","order":1004},
{"id":"p1-006","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why does a lower energy intensity not necessarily mean higher energy efficiency?","back":"Energy intensity also depends on economic structure (share of services against heavy industry), climate, geography, lifestyle, exchange rates and how much non-commercial energy is counted. It is not only technical efficiency.","explain":"A service-led economy can have low energy intensity even with inefficient equipment. A country with a large steel or cement base can show high intensity even with efficient plants. Energy intensity is therefore only a rough proxy for efficiency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S6","order":1005},
{"id":"p1-007","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why is GDP converted using Purchasing Power Parity (PPP) when comparing energy intensity across countries?","back":"PPP values every country's GDP at a uniform price level. The comparison then reflects differences in real economic volume, not market exchange-rate distortions.","explain":"Market exchange rates undervalue the output of developing countries, where many goods and services are cheaper. GDP at market exchange rates therefore makes their energy intensity look much higher than it really is. PPP-based GDP gives a fairer comparison.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S6; BEE 2025 P1 Q35","order":1006},
{"id":"p1-008","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"How are energy conservation and energy efficiency related?","back":"Energy efficiency means using less energy for the same output or service (lower energy per unit). Energy conservation means reducing overall energy consumption, which can include behaviour change or doing without.","explain":"Efficiency is about technology and how well equipment performs, such as an efficient motor doing the same work with less input. Conservation is broader: it includes efficiency and also avoiding waste, such as switching off idle equipment. Both cut demand, and efficiency does not require reducing comfort or output.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q36","order":1007},
{"id":"p1-009","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why is energy saved through Demand Side Management (DSM) treated like new supply-side capacity?","back":"Each MW of demand avoided by DSM does the same job as adding a MW of generation (a 'negawatt'), and it costs much less than building new power plants.","explain":"DSM programmes run by utilities or DISCOMs shift or reduce load, especially at peak, through efficient appliances, time-of-day tariffs and load management. The aims are to lower peak demand, defer capital spending on capacity, and improve the load factor. Increasing generator load to meet the peak is NOT a DSM objective.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q1; BEE 2024 P1 Q10","order":1008},
{"id":"p1-010","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"What was the main aim of India's Accelerated Power Development and Reform Programme (APDRP)?","back":"To reduce Aggregate Technical & Commercial (AT&C) losses in distribution through energy accounting and audits, metering, and strengthening of the sub-transmission and distribution network.","explain":"AT&C losses combine technical losses (I²R in lines and transformers) with commercial losses (theft, unmetered supply, billing and collection gaps). APDRP and its restructured successor (R-APDRP) funded IT-based energy accounting and network upgrades for state utilities.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q2","order":1009},
{"id":"p1-011","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"fact","front":"Why was Availability Based Tariff (ABT) introduced in India?","back":"To improve grid discipline and frequency control. It does this with a tariff of three parts: a capacity (fixed) charge, an energy (variable) charge, and an Unscheduled Interchange (UI) charge linked to frequency.","explain":"Under ABT, a generator is paid its capacity charge for the availability it declares, and its energy charge for energy actually scheduled. Deviations from schedule are priced by grid frequency: over-drawing at low frequency is expensive. This rewards keeping to schedule and supporting frequency.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q32","order":1010},
{"id":"p1-012","paper":1,"topic":"Energy scenario","kind":"problem","front":"Country X consumes 500 Mtoe of energy and has a GDP of US$ 2.5 trillion. Country Y consumes 300 Mtoe and has a GDP of US$ 1.2 trillion. Calculate the energy intensity of each in toe per million US$ and state which uses energy more productively.","back":"X: 200 toe per million US$; Y: 250 toe per million US$. Country X has the lower energy intensity.","explain":"Energy intensity = energy ÷ GDP, so a lower value means more GDP per unit of energy. Before concluding that X is more energy efficient, remember that economic structure, climate and PPP effects also affect intensity.","steps":[{"prompt":"Express GDP in million US$.","answer":"X: 2.5 trillion = 2,500,000 million US$; Y: 1.2 trillion = 1,200,000 million US$.","explain":"1 trillion = 10⁶ million."},{"prompt":"Express energy in toe.","answer":"X: 500 × 10⁶ toe; Y: 300 × 10⁶ toe.","explain":"1 Mtoe = 10⁶ toe."},{"prompt":"Compute energy intensity = energy ÷ GDP.","answer":"X: 500×10⁶ ÷ 2.5×10⁶ = 200 toe/million US$; Y: 300×10⁶ ÷ 1.2×10⁶ = 250 toe/million US$.","explain":"EI = Energy consumption ÷ GDP."},{"prompt":"Interpret the result.","answer":"X has the lower EI, so it produces more GDP per toe.","explain":"Lower intensity suggests better productivity of energy use, but is not by itself proof of technical efficiency."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 S2 (modified)","order":1011},
{"id":"p1-013","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"1 tonne of oil equivalent (toe) equals how many kcal and MJ?","back":"1 toe = 10⁷ kcal = 41,868 MJ (41.868 GJ).","explain":"toe is the common unit BEE uses to add up different fuels; for example Designated Consumer thresholds and PAT targets are in toe. Convert each fuel to kcal (quantity × GCV) and divide by 10⁷. For example, 2,500 kg of coal at 4,000 kcal/kg = 10⁷ kcal = 1 toe.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q6; BEE 2024 P1 Q33","order":1012},
{"id":"p1-014","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What are the energy equivalents of 1 kWh and 1 kcal?","back":"1 kWh = 860 kcal = 3,600 kJ (3.6 MJ); 1 kcal = 4.187 kJ.","explain":"These conversions are used in almost every Paper 1 numerical. To convert a thermal load in kcal/h to electrical kW, divide by 860. To convert electrical energy to toe, multiply kWh by 860 and divide by 10⁷.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S5, S8","order":1013},
{"id":"p1-015","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Which of these is NOT equal to 1 atm: 101.3 kPa, 10,332 mmWC, 14.7 psi, 0.98 kg/cm²?","back":"0.98 kg/cm² is wrong. 1 atm ≈ 1.033 kg/cm² (101.325 kPa = 10,332 mmWC = 14.7 psi = 760 mmHg).","explain":"1 kg/cm² ≈ 0.98 bar, which is easy to confuse. Remember 1 atm = 1.01325 bar ≈ 1.033 kg/cm², slightly more than 1 kg/cm².","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q2","order":1014},
{"id":"p1-016","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"State the relation between gauge pressure, absolute (system) pressure and atmospheric pressure.","back":"Gauge pressure = Absolute (system) pressure − Atmospheric pressure, i.e. pg = ps − pa.","explain":"Pressure gauges read relative to the surrounding atmosphere, so a gauge open to air reads zero. Steam tables use absolute pressure, so add about 1.013 bar to a gauge reading before looking up properties.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q31","order":1015},
{"id":"p1-017","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What property does 'Redwood seconds' measure?","back":"Viscosity. It is the time in seconds for a fixed volume of oil to flow through a standard orifice of the Redwood viscometer.","explain":"Viscosity matters for fuel oils because it sets the pumping and atomisation temperature. Furnace oil is preheated to bring its viscosity down to the level the burner needs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q3","order":1016},
{"id":"p1-018","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Which common substance has the highest specific heat, and what is its value?","back":"Water, at about 1 kcal/kg°C (4.187 kJ/kg·K). It is far higher than metals such as iron (~0.11), mercury (~0.033) or lead (~0.03).","explain":"Water's high specific heat makes it an excellent heat-transfer and storage medium, used in cooling towers, hot-water systems and chilled water. Heating 500 litres of water (500 kg) by 6°C needs 500 × 1 × 6 = 3,000 kcal, which is about 12,560 kJ.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q7, Q10; BEE 2024 P1 Q8","order":1017},
{"id":"p1-019","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What is the formula for sensible heat, and how does latent heat differ?","back":"Sensible heat Q = m × Cp × ΔT, which changes temperature without a change of phase. Latent heat Q = m × L, which changes phase (e.g. boiling or condensing) at constant temperature.","explain":"Example: cooling 2,000 kg of water by 10°C removes 2,000 × 1 × 10 = 20,000 kcal of sensible heat. Condensing steam releases its latent heat (about 540 kcal/kg at atmospheric pressure), which is why steam is such an effective heating medium.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q10","order":1018},
{"id":"p1-020","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What is the dryness fraction (x) of dry saturated steam, and what does x < 1 mean?","back":"x = 1 for dry saturated steam (BEE also gives x = 1 for superheated steam). x < 1 means wet steam that carries water droplets: x = mass of dry steam ÷ total mass.","explain":"Wet steam has less usable enthalpy per kg (hf + x·hfg). It also causes erosion and poorer heat transfer, so steam traps and separators are used to keep steam dry.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q39","order":1019},
{"id":"p1-021","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How do you calculate the number of moles in a given mass? (e.g. 72 g or 54 g of water)","back":"Moles = mass ÷ molecular weight. Water (H₂O) has a molecular weight of 18, so 72 g = 4 mol and 54 g = 3 mol.","explain":"Mole calculations underpin combustion stoichiometry. For example, C + O₂ → CO₂ means 12 kg of carbon gives 44 kg of CO₂, so emissions = carbon × 44/12.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q9; BEE 2025 P1 Q46","order":1020},
{"id":"p1-022","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What is the mean molecular weight of air (77% N₂, 23% O₂ by weight)?","back":"About 28.8 (≈ 29) g/mol. Calculation: 1 ÷ (0.77/28 + 0.23/32) ≈ 28.8.","explain":"When composition is given by weight, use the harmonic mean: M = 1 ÷ Σ(wᵢ/Mᵢ). By volume, air is about 79% N₂ and 21% O₂, and the mole-weighted average gives the same ≈ 28.8–29.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q50","order":1021},
{"id":"p1-023","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Which fuel has the largest difference between GCV and NCV, and why?","back":"Natural gas. Its high hydrogen content forms a lot of water vapour on combustion, and the latent heat of that vapour is counted in GCV but not in NCV.","explain":"GCV (HHV) assumes the water vapour in flue gas is condensed and its latent heat recovered. NCV (LHV) assumes it leaves as vapour. Fuels rich in hydrogen (natural gas, LPG) or moisture show the biggest gap. BEE efficiency calculations conventionally use GCV.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q27","order":1022},
{"id":"p1-024","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Rank LPG, diesel, furnace oil and bagasse by calorific value. Which is lowest?","back":"Bagasse is lowest (roughly 2,200–2,300 kcal/kg as fired, because of about 50% moisture). LPG is highest (~11,000+ kcal/kg). Diesel and furnace oil are about 10,000–10,800 kcal/kg.","explain":"Biomass fuels such as bagasse and rice husk have low calorific value because of moisture and ash. They still matter as low-cost renewable fuels for sugar mills and cogeneration.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q11","order":1023},
{"id":"p1-025","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What two measurements are needed to find the relative humidity of air with a psychrometer?","back":"Dry Bulb Temperature (DBT) and Wet Bulb Temperature (WBT). RH is then read from a psychrometric chart. A sling psychrometer measures both.","explain":"DBT indicates the sensible heat content of the air-water vapour mixture. The wet-bulb depression (DBT − WBT) shows how dry the air is. When the depression is zero, no evaporation is possible and RH = 100%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q14, Q21, Q32","order":1024},
{"id":"p1-026","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What happens to the relative humidity when air is heated without changing its moisture content (absolute humidity)?","back":"RH decreases, because warmer air can hold more moisture at saturation while the actual moisture stays the same.","explain":"This is the principle of pre-heating air before a dryer: it lowers RH and increases the air's capacity to pick up moisture. Cooling air at constant moisture raises RH until it reaches 100% at the dew point.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q26","order":1025},
{"id":"p1-027","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Define power factor. What is the PF of a circuit with resistive and inductive load?","back":"PF = kW ÷ kVA = cos φ. A resistive + inductive (R-L) circuit has a lagging PF of less than 1. Also, kVA² = kW² + kVAr².","explain":"Inductive loads such as motors and transformers draw reactive power (kVAr) for their magnetic fields. That raises kVA and current for the same kW, which increases I²R losses and kVA-based demand charges. Capacitors supply kVAr locally to improve PF. Only a pure resistance gives unity PF.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q24","order":1026},
{"id":"p1-028","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"An induction motor nameplate says 30 kW, 85% efficiency. How much power does it draw at full load?","back":"35.29 kW. The nameplate kW is shaft output, so input = 30 ÷ 0.85 = 35.29 kW.","explain":"Motor ratings always state mechanical output. Electrical input = output ÷ efficiency. Audits compare measured input kW with rated input to estimate percentage loading.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q30","order":1027},
{"id":"p1-029","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"How does wind turbine power vary with wind speed, and what is the formula?","back":"P = ½ ρ A V³, so power varies with the cube of wind speed: tripling the speed gives 27× the power.","explain":"For example, ρ = 1.2 kg/m³, A = 200 m² and V = 8 m/s gives 0.5 × 1.2 × 200 × 512 ≈ 61.4 kW available in the wind. A turbine captures only part of this, with the Betz limit at 59.3%. Yaw control turns the rotor to face the wind, and pitch control adjusts the blade angle.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q16, Q25; BEE 2025 P1 L3","order":1028},
{"id":"p1-030","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Roughly what voltage does a single silicon PV cell produce, and what is its working principle?","back":"About 0.5 V. It works by the photovoltaic effect: photons create electron-hole pairs at a p-n junction, which produces DC current.","explain":"Cells are connected in series and parallel in modules to reach usable voltage and power. Cell efficiency = maximum power output ÷ (insolation × cell area), so it does not depend on the inverter. An inverter converts DC to AC.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q20, Q22; BEE 2025 P1 L3","order":1029},
{"id":"p1-031","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What are the reactants and by-product in a hydrogen fuel cell?","back":"Hydrogen combines electrochemically with oxygen to generate electricity, and water is the by-product (plus heat).","explain":"A fuel cell converts chemical energy directly into electricity without combustion, so it is not limited by Carnot efficiency. Hydrogen is oxidised at the anode, and oxygen is reduced at the cathode.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q35","order":1030},
{"id":"p1-032","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"What is the composition of producer gas, and how is gasifier efficiency calculated?","back":"Producer gas is mainly CO, H₂ and CH₄ (combustible), diluted with N₂ and CO₂. Gasifier efficiency = (gas volume × gas CV) ÷ (biomass mass × biomass CV).","explain":"Example: 1 kg of wood at 4,000 kcal/kg giving 2 m³ of gas at 1,000 kcal/m³ → 2,000/4,000 = 50% efficiency. Producer gas has a low CV (about 1,000–1,200 kcal/Nm³) because it contains a lot of nitrogen from the air.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q21, Q49","order":1031},
{"id":"p1-033","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"fact","front":"Solar radiation reaching the earth consists of which bands?","back":"Ultraviolet, visible and infrared radiation.","explain":"Roughly half of solar energy is in the infrared and most of the rest is visible light. A small part is ultraviolet, most of which is absorbed by stratospheric ozone.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q25","order":1032},
{"id":"p1-034","paper":1,"topic":"Basics of energy","kind":"problem","front":"A plant uses in one year: 12 million kWh of grid electricity; 1,500 kL of furnace oil (density 0.93 kg/L, GCV 10,200 kcal/kg); and 8,000 t of coal (GCV 4,000 kcal/kg). It produces 20,000 t of product. Find the total annual energy use in toe and the specific energy consumption in toe/t. Take 1 kWh = 860 kcal.","back":"Total ≈ 5,655 toe per year; SEC ≈ 0.283 toe/t of product.","explain":"Converting every energy source to toe through kcal is exactly how a plant checks whether it qualifies as a Designated Consumer, and how SEC is reported under PAT. Coal is the biggest share here, about 57% of the total.","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert electricity to toe.","answer":"12×10⁶ kWh × 860 = 1.032×10¹⁰ kcal → ÷10⁷ = 1,032 toe","explain":"1 kWh = 860 kcal; 1 toe = 10⁷ kcal."},{"prompt":"Convert furnace oil to toe.","answer":"1,500 kL × 1,000 L × 0.93 = 13,95,000 kg; × 10,200 = 1.4229×10¹⁰ kcal → 1,422.9 toe","explain":"Mass = volume × density; energy = mass × GCV."},{"prompt":"Convert coal to toe.","answer":"8,000 t = 8×10⁶ kg × 4,000 = 3.2×10¹⁰ kcal → 3,200 toe","explain":"Energy = mass × GCV."},{"prompt":"Add up and compute SEC.","answer":"Total = 1,032 + 1,422.9 + 3,200 = 5,654.9 toe; SEC = 5,654.9 ÷ 20,000 = 0.283 toe/t","explain":"SEC = total energy ÷ production."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 L4 (modified)","order":1033},
{"id":"p1-035","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"When was the Energy Conservation Act enacted, and when did BEE come into existence?","back":"The EC Act was enacted in 2001 (in force from 1 March 2002). The Bureau of Energy Efficiency (BEE) was set up under it on 1 March 2002, under the Ministry of Power.","explain":"BEE is the statutory body responsible for implementing the EC Act. Its mission is to develop policies and strategies, with emphasis on self-regulation and market principles, to reduce the energy intensity of the Indian economy. It does not set tariffs or give renewable capital subsidies.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q40; BEE 2025 P1 Q40","order":1034},
{"id":"p1-036","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Who are 'Designated Consumers' (DCs) under the EC Act, and what must they do?","back":"Large energy users in energy-intensive industries and establishments notified in the Act's Schedule. They must appoint or designate a certified energy manager, get mandatory energy audits done by an accredited energy auditor, report energy consumption, and meet the specified energy consumption norms.","explain":"Scheduled sectors include thermal power, cement, iron & steel, aluminium, fertilizer, pulp & paper, textile, chlor-alkali, petroleum refineries, railways and DISCOMs, among others. The threshold for each sector is notified in tonnes of oil equivalent per year. Non-compliance attracts penalties.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q37; BEE 2024 P1 L4","order":1035},
{"id":"p1-037","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What data do you need to check whether a plant qualifies as a Designated Consumer?","back":"Annual consumption of every energy source (grid electricity in kWh, coal in t, furnace oil and HSD in litres or kL, LPG or natural gas in kg or m³) together with each fuel's calorific value (and density for liquids). Convert everything to toe and compare with the notified threshold for that sector.","explain":"Total toe = Σ(quantity × GCV) ÷ 10⁷ kcal. Electricity is converted using 860 kcal/kWh. Include all purchased and self-generated energy inputs.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 L4","order":1036},
{"id":"p1-038","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"Name the main measures empowered by the Energy Conservation Act 2001.","back":"Standards & Labelling of appliances, mandatory energy audits and energy managers for Designated Consumers, energy consumption norms, the Energy Conservation Building Code, and creation of BEE, State Designated Agencies and Energy Conservation Funds.","explain":"The Central Government acts through BEE, and State Governments act through State Designated Agencies (SDAs), which enforce the Act at state level. Energy Saving Certificates were added by the 2010 amendment and form the basis of PAT.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q41","order":1037},
{"id":"p1-039","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What were the key changes made by the Energy Conservation (Amendment) Act 2022?","back":"Power to set up a Carbon Credit Trading Scheme; mandatory minimum share of non-fossil (green hydrogen, biomass, renewable) energy for designated consumers; extension of the building code to large residential buildings as the Energy Conservation and Sustainable Building Code; Standards & Labelling extended to vehicles and vessels; enlarged BEE Governing Council; SERCs to adjudicate penalties.","explain":"The amendment moved the Act from energy efficiency alone towards decarbonisation and India's climate commitments. It gives the legal basis for the Indian Carbon Market, which issues carbon credit certificates.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":1038},
{"id":"p1-040","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What penalty applies for non-compliance with EC Act provisions, as amended?","back":"Up to ₹10 lakh, plus up to ₹10,000 for each day the failure continues. A Designated Consumer that fails its energy consumption norms is also liable to an additional penalty linked to the price of each excess metric toe consumed.","explain":"These penalties are meant to make compliance cheaper than violation. Under the 2022 amendment, penalties are adjudicated by the State Electricity Regulatory Commission.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1039},
{"id":"p1-041","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What is the objective of BEE's Standards & Labelling (S&L) programme?","back":"To give consumers an informed choice about the energy and cost-saving potential of appliances, through star labels (1 to 5 stars; more stars means more efficient).","explain":"Launched in 2006, S&L covers appliances such as ACs, refrigerators, motors, distribution transformers and LED lamps, some under mandatory labelling. It pushes the market towards efficient products, and thresholds are tightened over time.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q29","order":1040},
{"id":"p1-042","paper":1,"topic":"Energy Conservation Act","kind":"fact","front":"What is the purpose of the Energy Conservation Building Code (ECBC)?","back":"To set minimum energy-performance standards for new commercial buildings (connected load ≥ 100 kW or contract demand ≥ 120 kVA), covering envelope, HVAC, lighting, hot water and electrical systems.","explain":"ECBC was first launched in 2007 and revised in 2017. A residential code (Eco-Niwas Samhita) followed, and the 2022 amendment renamed it the Energy Conservation and Sustainable Building Code. Daylight harvesting means automatically dimming artificial lighting according to available daylight, and is a typical ECBC measure.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q41, Q42","order":1041},
{"id":"p1-043","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"How does the EC Act define an energy audit?","back":"The verification, monitoring and analysis of energy use, including a technical report with recommendations for improving energy efficiency, a cost-benefit analysis, and an action plan to reduce energy consumption.","explain":"The definition has three parts: measurement/analysis, recommendations with economics, and an action plan. An audit is only useful if it leads to implementation, which is why the report must quantify savings and costs.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1042},
{"id":"p1-044","paper":1,"topic":"Energy management & audit","kind":"fact","front":"What is the difference between a preliminary and a detailed energy audit?","back":"A preliminary (walk-through) audit is quick and uses existing data to set the consumption baseline, find obvious savings and no-cost or low-cost measures, and set priorities. A detailed audit is a comprehensive, instrument-based study of all major systems, with a full energy balance and costed projects.","explain":"A detailed audit is done in three phases: pre-audit, audit and post-audit. It gives the most accurate estimate of savings and costs, and includes a detailed project implementation plan.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1043}
]$cards$::jsonb);
